    "reasoning": "Each final answer is mathematically correct and supported by its own captured worked solution. No submitted grades or earlier answers were supplied. Tangent approximations use L(x)=f(a)+f'(a)(x-a): q-4868 gives 0.5+5(0.1)=1; q-115593 has slope 2 and gives 2-π/2; q-4814 has slope 6 and gives 4; q-115265 gives 2-2(-0.2)=2.4, an underestimate because the function is concave up; q-346720 gives 2+0.5(0.4)=2.2, an overestimate because the function is concave down. For q-128382, (0,-3,0) lies on the plane and (3,5,0) is its normal. For q-336218, the line direction (0,-5,4) is the plane normal, giving -5(y-2)+4(z-3)=0, equivalent to 5y-4z+2=0. For q-74650, dependent equations require k+1=3, hence k=2. For q-131720, the second equation is 3/2 times the first, leaving two free variables, so only III is true. For q-75519, the tangent's dot product with (0,1) is t/√(1+t²), which vanishes only at t=0, giving (1,1). For q-165039, parallelism requires 6t+9=3(3t+4), hence t=-1 and the point (-5/2,-6). Its source contains an authored dimensional typo referring once to the three-component vector ⟨0,1,3⟩; the subsequent equations correctly use ⟨1,3⟩, and the final answer remains correct. For q-114010, equal integration limits give 0. For q-114016, reversing the known limits gives ∫₁³f=6, so the answer is 8(2)-6=10. For q-73342, ∫₋₂¹f=5/2, so the answer is 5(3)+4(5/2)=25. For q-114153, ∫₁⁴f=-6, so ∫₋₁⁴f=6-6=0. For q-4726, ∫₀⁷g=10, so ∫₀³(f-g)=18-10-4=4. All final answers agree with MA; the dimensional typo is confined to intermediate prose.",
